0465: no scroll event carries a gesture phase; a touch wakes the sleeping display; a scripted gesture test.

S6i 2026-11-16 (hands-on, sweetpad=1 as 0460 made it): "everything is crashing, even Finder". Finder (twice) and
System Settings aborted in AppKit's _NSScrollingConcurrentVBLMonitor (_run_monitor_thread -> os_crash) 10-25 s after
two-finger scrolls. Scroll events with trackpad phases put AppKit into its concurrent-scrolling mode, which waits for
the display's vertical blank; nothing delivers one here. And for the first 49 s touches "did nothing": macOS had put
the display to sleep after 10 idle minutes (our driver keeps the panel lit with a5fb_keep_on, so it looked frozen);
pointer events from this service do not wake it, the first key of the on-screen keyboard did.
- sweetpad is a bit mask: 1 = three-finger swipes (+ the leftover-finger rule), 2 = two-finger scrolling in points
  with our own inertia, sent as plain pixel-unit scroll events. No phase, no momentum flags, in any mode.
- sweet_fb_off_requested (SweetFramebuffer.cpp RequestPowerChange): a touch-down while macOS has the display off taps
  Shift through the keyboard service (at most once a second).
- sweetpadtest=N: N s after boot scripted gestures through SweetHIDTouch::touch: two-finger scrolls, three-finger
  swipes, waits of 20-25 s, twice; a third round 700 s after boot (after the 10-minute display sleep).
tools/macos/padtest checks every mode and that no event has phase or momentum bits. Kernel seq2j = seq2i + 0465.

